Ladybird rasterizes repeated vector backgrounds and cached nested
SVG display lists at a resolution that includes the destination
transform. It keeps the recorded resolution as a lower bound, and
includes the raster resolution in cache lookup. The same SVG resource
can therefore render at different scales without reusing a magnified
bitmap recorded for another destination.

Blink derives its raster scale from the screen-space transform in
LayerImpl::GetIdealContentsScale, and applies it to picture tilings
in PictureLayerImpl::UpdateIdealScales. WebKit sizes SVG pattern
buffers from the drawing context transform in SVGImage::drawPattern.
Its GraphicsLayerCA::updateRootRelativeScale accumulates ancestor
transform scales for the backing resolution in updateContentsScale.
Gecko bypasses the SVG surface cache in VectorImage::Draw when it
records drawing commands for WebRender. PictureInstance::assign_surface
then uses local-to-device and parent-surface scales for its surfaces.
These paths preserve vector detail at the final scale, which is the
behavior Ladybird matches for smooth vector-image magnification.

Nearest-neighbor tiles keep their recorded pixel grid. Chrome matches
that behavior in the fractional-transform, pixelated-background
control: Enlarging its vector raster changes the edge coverage before
the nearest-neighbor sampling, and no longer produces the same pixels.

Repeated-tile enlargement is limited to 16384 pixels per dimension
and 128 MiB, while keeping the recorded tile as its minimum size.
Perspective tiles keep their recorded resolution. Nested-list rasters
retain the 128 MiB per-raster limit, 512 MiB cache budget, and direct
replay fallback. Rotated, skewed, mirrored, or destination-reading
nested lists still replay directly without caching a raster image.
The sampled scale determines the raster size, so an animated scale
selects or creates a cached image as its raster dimensions change.

Chrome renders the reported animated curves sharply at four times
their layout size. A reference with an animated repeated SVG matches
untransformed vector backgrounds at uniform and nonuniform scales.
The GPU regression reuses one nested resource at several scales,
after a clipped cache warmup, and compares complete images against
direct vector replay. Both regressions fail without the patch.
